<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
	What the hardened runtime gives back to Caskade's executable. Release builds
	are signed with the hardened runtime (scripts/macos/sign.sh), which notarization
	requires; this asks for the one thing it would take away.

	allow-unsigned-executable-memory: plugins are wasm components that wasmtime
	compiles with Cranelift at run time. It writes the code, then makes it
	executable with mprotect, without MAP_JIT, so allow-jit doesn't cover it. CI
	runs a hardened build of crates/caskade-component-cache/examples/hardened_jit.rs
	with and without this file to check it is still needed and still enough.

	Nothing else: the game and video windows' WebKit runs its JIT in WebKit's own
	processes, the keychain needs no entitlement outside the sandbox, and games
	are separate programs with their own signatures. 7zz is signed with none.
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<key>com.apple.security.cs.allow-unsigned-executable-memory</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
